          "title": "2.4 Political Parties and Party Systems",
          "image": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/5547056/pexels-photo-5547056.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
          "video": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hFrSktJay4M",
          "content": "Political Party: An organized group of people who share similar political aims and opinions, and who seek to influence or control government policy and personnel by nominating and electing candidates to public office.\n\nFunctions of Political Parties:\n\nRecruit and Nominate Candidates: Select and support individuals to run for elected office.\n\nEducate and Mobilize Voters: Inform citizens about issues, stimulate political interest, encourage participation (voting).\n\nFormulate Policies and Programs (Party Platform/Manifesto): Develop sets of proposals to address societal problems and guide government action if elected.\n\nOrganize and Run Government: If a party wins an election, it forms the government (or part of a coalition government) and works to implement its policies.\n\nProvide Opposition and Scrutiny: Parties not in power (opposition) critically examine government actions, hold it accountable, and offer alternative policies.\n\nAggregate and Articulate Interests: Bring together diverse interests within society and present them in a coherent way.\n\nFacilitate Political Socialization and Participation.\n\nPromote political stability (by channeling conflict through organized means).\n\nParty Systems: The system of interaction resulting from inter-party competition in a particular country. Classified by the number of dominant parties.\n\nOne-Party System:\n\nOnly one political party is legally allowed to exist or hold effective power. Other parties are banned or suppressed.\n\nCitizens have no real choice of parties in elections.\n\nGovernment is typically authoritarian or totalitarian.\n\nExamples: China (Communist Party), Cuba (Communist Party), former Soviet Union.\n\nTwo-Party System:\n\nTwo major political parties dominate the political landscape and alternate in power, although other smaller parties may exist.\n\nIt is difficult for third parties to win significant representation.\n\nOften associated with electoral systems like First-Past-the-Post.\n\nAdvantages: Can lead to stable governments, clear choice for voters.\n\nDisadvantages: Can limit voter choice, lead to polarization, neglect minority interests.\n\nExamples: United States (Democrats, Republicans), United Kingdom (historically Conservative, Labour, though now more multi-party elements).\n\nMulti-Party System:\n\nThree or more political parties have a realistic chance of gaining power, either individually or as part of a coalition government.\n\nCommon in countries with proportional representation electoral systems.\n\nAdvantages: Offers wider voter choice, better representation of diverse interests and ideologies, may encourage consensus-building (in coalitions).\n\nDisadvantages: Coalition governments can be unstable and prone to frequent changes; decision-making can be slower or more complex.\n\nExamples: Germany, Italy, Canada, India, many European democracies.\n\nDominant-Party System: One party consistently wins elections and remains in power for a long period, even though other parties are legally allowed to compete (e.g., ANC in post-apartheid South Africa for a period, LDP in Japan for many years).\n\nThe type of party system is influenced by factors like electoral laws, historical development, social cleavages, and political culture.\n\n(Suggested insertion: A table listing functions of political parties. Another table comparing One-Party, Two-Party, and Multi-Party systems with characteristics, pros/cons, and examples.)",
